content:
`startFrom` is the third, optional field on a `ProcessorRecord`:

```typescript
type ProcessorRecord = {
  processor: IProcessor;
  filter: ProcessorFilter;
  startFrom?: "beginning" | "current";
};
```

Its semantics are narrower than the name suggests. It is consulted **only when a processor is first created and no cursor row exists yet**:

- `"beginning"` (the default) starts the cursor at ordinal 0, so the processor backfills the drive's full history.
- `"current"` starts the cursor at the manager's current ordinal, so the processor sees only operations from now on.

Once a cursor row exists, `startFrom` is **ignored** — the persisted cursor wins. Restarting the reactor never re-runs `startFrom`. This is the point most people get wrong: flipping a deployed processor from `"current"` to `"beginning"` will not backfill it, because its cursor row already exists. To actually replay, you must call `tracked.retry()` (which re-activates and re-runs backfill) or clear the cursor row; changing the declaration alone is inert.

Cursors live in the `ProcessorCursor` table keyed by `processorId` (`${factoryId}:${driveId}:${index}`) and survive restarts. On startup the manager rehydrates every cursor and catch-up replays the gap.

The advance rule has its own subtlety. On each batch the manager filters to operations with `ordinal > lastOrdinal`, applies the filter, calls `onOperations` on the matches, and on success advances `lastOrdinal` to **the maximum ordinal in the batch, including unmatched operations**. The cursor therefore moves forward even when nothing matched — a narrowly filtered processor does not accumulate an ever-growing backlog of skipped work.

Backfill uses `operationIndex.getSinceOrdinal(lastOrdinal)`, paging through history, filtering each page, calling `onOperations`, advancing and persisting the cursor per page, and following the continuation until exhausted. That path runs in three situations: registering a factory against a drive that already has history, restarting after downtime, and `retry()` on an errored processor.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/05-Processors.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
